    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{n(n-3)}{2}$\n\nCount $n-3$ diagonals from each vertex, then halve because each diagonal is counted twice.",
      "worked": "From each vertex, exclude the vertex itself and its two adjacent vertices. This leaves $n-3$ diagonals from each of the $n$ vertices. Each diagonal is counted at both endpoints.\n$$\\text{Number of diagonals}=\\frac{n(n-3)}{2}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
